 'Application Whitelisting / Blacklisting':{family:'execution_allow_block_policy_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify the contrast between allow-policy and deny-policy execution controls, without inferring perfect enforcement or that approved software is harmless.',contrasts:['An allow policy starts from authorized entries; a block policy targets prohibited entries and may admit unknown software. Audit mode is not blocking enforcement.','Matching attributes, permitted interpreters, configuration weaknesses and maintenance affect bypasses and effectiveness. The compound card is one vocabulary prompt, not two independent control assessments.']},
 Sandboxing:{family:'restricted_execution_environment_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify restricted execution intended to make host impact harder. The delivered cannot easily wording describes risk reduction rather than an absolute no-impact or no-escape guarantee.',contrasts:['Authorized resources, broker interfaces and policy still matter. The NIST definitions restrict permissions; Chromium examples depend on the OS and do not protect against all system-component bugs.','No probability of escape, host immunity or safe execution of a particular malicious sample is established. This is terminology only; a VM alone does not establish a correctly configured sandbox.']},
 'Vulnerability Scanning':{family:'automated_weakness_detection_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify automated discovery/reporting of known weaknesses as the scanning role, not exhaustive coverage or confirmation that every reported weakness is real.',contrasts:['False positives and false negatives require interpretation; a clean scan does not establish absence of vulnerabilities. Scanner scope, credentials and signatures influence coverage.','Scanning differs from exploitation, prioritization and remediation. No scanner, credential, device or vulnerability outcome is observed.']},
 'False Positive':{family:'reported_absent_condition_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify a reported condition that is absent in the relevant ground truth and scope, rather than an analyst simply deciding to dismiss an alert.',contrasts:['This differs from a true positive and from a false negative, where a present condition is missed. The definition is conditional on a specified detector outcome and actual condition.','No alert or ground-truth validation occurs; this card does not approve alert suppression, risk acceptance or a measured scanner error rate.']},
 'False Negative':{family:'missed_present_condition_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify failure to detect a condition that is actually present, within the detector task being evaluated.',contrasts:['Absence of an alert is not proof of a false negative or a clean system without ground truth. A false positive instead reports an absent condition.','Vulnerability scans and malicious-activity detectors are distinct examples; no detector coverage, incident or error rate is validated.']},
 'Patch Management':{family:'tested_update_lifecycle_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify testing and deployment of corrective software updates as part of patch management; the short card is not a complete enterprise lifecycle or assurance of successful remediation.',contrasts:['Planning also involves identifying, prioritizing, acquiring and verifying updates. Testing balances exposure against availability and may still miss problems.','Scope can include applications, operating systems and firmware, with dependencies and end-of-support limits. No patch installation, rollback, compatibility or resulting vulnerability removal is observed.']},
 'Compensating Control':{defer:true,family:'alternative_comparable_protection_vocabulary',qualification:'HOLD: the delivered alternative-when-primary-unavailable definition omits comparable protective effectiveness and could treat any available substitute as compensation.',contrasts:['The inspected NIST definition requires equivalent or comparable protection in its baseline context. Unavailability of immediate patching alone does not establish that an alternative compensates for the missing control.','Risk evaluation, rationale, scope and applicable requirements matter. No equivalence, exception, compliance or owner approval is established; the learner sentence is not rewritten.']},
 'Provisioning & Deprovisioning':{family:'account_permission_lifecycle_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify creation, modification and removal of accounts/permissions as lifecycle work, without promising immediate or complete revocation in every connected system.',contrasts:['Deprovisioning can involve disabling access as well as removal; retention, sessions, tokens, shared credentials and downstream systems need separate handling.','The inspected account-management requirement distinguishes policy-driven actions and organization-defined notification timing. No real removal, synchronization, departure event or timing SLA is observed.']},
 'Least Privilege Principle':{family:'minimum_required_authorization_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify minimizing user/process/system access to what the assigned function needs, without asserting a particular role or implementation satisfies the principle.',contrasts:['Role assignment, authentication and least privilege are distinct; a valid login or RBAC design can still grant excess privileges.','Job scope, resource/action boundaries, effective/inherited rights and ongoing review determine implementation. No actual permissions, privilege removal or policy enforcement is verified.']}
